=== HINT 1: Open Run key ===
HKEY hKey = NULL;
LONG rc = RegOpenKeyExA(HKEY_CURRENT_USER,
    "Software\\Microsoft\\Windows\\CurrentVersion\\Run",
    0, KEY_SET_VALUE, &hKey);

=== HINT 2: Write value ===
rc = RegSetValueExA(hKey, VALUE_NAME, 0, REG_SZ,
    (BYTE*)exe_path, (DWORD)(strlen(exe_path) + 1));

=== HINT 3: Verify, then clean up (no test persistence left behind) ===
char verify[MAX_PATH]; DWORD vlen = sizeof(verify), vtype;
RegQueryValueExA(hKey, VALUE_NAME, NULL, &vtype, (BYTE*)verify, &vlen);
printf("Verified: %s\n", verify);
rc = RegDeleteValueA(hKey, VALUE_NAME);
if (rc == ERROR_SUCCESS) printf("(cleanup: run key removed)\n");
RegCloseKey(hKey);

=== KEY INSIGHT ===
HKCU Run: fires at user login, no admin needed.
HKLM Run: all users, requires admin, more suspicious.
RunOnce: fires once, self-deletes after execution.
Stealthier persistence locations:
  HKCU\...\Explorer\Shell Folders\Startup - startup folder
  HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services - service (admin, survives reboots)
  HKCU\...\Policies\Explorer\Run - GPO-like, less monitored
  Scheduled tasks (rt09) - separate database, task scheduler API
  DLL hijacking in known paths - no registry write at all
Detection: Autoruns (Sysinternals), EDR registry write monitoring,
  Sysmon EventID 13 (registry value set).
